  "lines": [
    {
      "id": "hook",
      "text": "Three animals that basically refuse to die.",
      "hold_after": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "jelly1",
      "text": "First, the immortal jellyfish. It's smaller than your fingernail."
    },
    {
      "id": "jelly2",
      "text": "But when it gets old or hurt, it doesn't die. It turns back into a baby and starts its whole life over."
    },
    {
      "id": "jelly3",
      "text": "And then it can do it again. And again."
    },
    {
      "id": "shark1",
      "text": "Next, the Greenland shark."
    },
    {
      "id": "shark2",
      "text": "Scientists dated one at around 400 years old. It was probably alive before the United States even existed."
    },
    {
      "id": "shark3",
      "text": "It grows about one centimeter a year, and it isn't even an adult until it's around 150."
    },
    {
      "id": "tort1",
      "text": "Finally, Jonathan the tortoise."
    },
    {
      "id": "tort2",
      "text": "He hatched around 1832, before anyone had ever taken a photo of a person."
    },
    {
      "id": "tort3",
      "text": "He's about 190 years old now, the oldest known land animal alive. And he's still going."
    }
  ]
